### 8.1 What exists, and what each piece owns

| Piece | Owns |
|---|---|
| `pi/api/main.py` | public FastAPI app: `/api/status`, `/api/ready`, `/api/health` (liveness only), `/search`, `/board`, `/files`, `/ask` + `/api/ask/stream` + `/api/ask/status` (the helper: `public_ask` closes the page **and** the stream, 403 both); mounts the admin and public arcade routers; bumps `session_epoch` at start |
| `pi/api/ark_health.py` | **the only place that decides readiness**: path helpers read at call time, content facts, cached loopback probe, **nine** per-section builders each with `ready`/`reason`/`action`/`content` (**six** of them blocking — `ask` is the ninth and never blocks, because a box with no model is a supported state), measured `progress()`, measured `storage_breakdown()` + `card_facts()`, `_scan_copies()`/`copies()`/`copies_unreadable()`/`_age_hours()`/`_ago()` + `backup_section()` (the **Latest copy** row: never blocking, a `.partial` is not a copy, a copy the pages may not open is reported and never counted as "none", an unreadable age is prose and never `0`), `files_section()` counting the files a reader can **open** (`files`/`files_missing`/`uploads`, `files_missing: None` when the folder cannot be listed), `ask_section()`, `report()` |
| `pi/bin/ark-net-watch` | **the only place that decides the network after boot**: pure `decide()`, `--dry-run` observer, counters in `net-watch.state`, decisions in `net-watch.log`, acts by calling `ark-mode` |
| `pi/systemd/ark-net-watch.service` / `.timer` | the clock for the above: `oneshot` every 60 s after a 3-minute boot delay; it acts through `ark-mode`, never by itself |
| `pi/bin/ark-mode` | the one writer of `/etc/ark/mode.pinned` (`home`/`hotspot` pin, `auto` unpins, `hotspot --auto` leaves no pin) and the only thing the watchdog calls |
| `pi/api/ark_db.py` | one connection policy (`read()` / `tx()`, always closed), numbered migrations in one transaction, audit helper, install marker, `SCHEMA_VERSION` (**6**), and `synchronous=FULL` set on **every** connection opened (not just on the one a repair took) — §27: a save or a note the box said it kept is on the card before it says so |
| `pi/api/ark_security.py` | scrypt hashing, length-only policy (12…1024, reject never truncate), opaque sessions (SHA-256 digest of a 256-bit token), CSRF, per-account + per-IP throttling |
| `pi/api/ark_admin.py` | `/admin` routes: login, forced setup, password, accounts, reset/enable/disable, audit, logout, overview, **Community** (switches + board/file moderation), **Games** (shelf, licences, suggestions, hide/show, both arcade switches, **A game you already have** → `POST /admin/arcade/upload` + `/upload/add` + `/upload/discard`), **`/admin/health`** ("What is working") |
| `pi/api/ark_storage.py` | the only write path for shared files: display name vs stored name, spool in `drop/tmp`, `os.link` finalise, size cap + free-space reserve, orphan-temp reconcile; and `present_names()` — the one reader of "what is really in the folder" (a set, or `None` when it cannot be listed), used by the listing and by `ark_health` |
| `pi/api/ark_arcade.py` / `ark_arcade_install.py` / `ark_arcade_web.py` | the game shelf: shelf rules and bounded saves, the only game download path, and the public/player pages + save API. Player names are separate tables and a separate cookie; `:8445` is the only origin that can set a save or issue that cookie. A save's bytes are **content-addressed** (`blob_dir()`/`blob_name()`/`blob_path()`/`save_file()`/`_prune_blobs()`, §27) — never overwritten, so a row and its file cannot disagree. Full detail in §8.11 |
| `pi/api/ark_romdb.py` | the only reader of a game file's identity: `strip_headers()` (SNES copier header, iNES header, a `.smc`), `identify_path()`/`identify_bytes()`, `match()` against `/srv/ark/romdb.sqlite`, `game_by_identity()` (these bytes under another name), `title_from_filename()`/`tags_of()`/`region_of()` when there is no catalogue, `stats()`, `may_reuse()`. Never refuses anything |
| `pi/api/ark_rommeta.py` | the words for a recognised file: `prefill()` (title, console, facts, sentence, confidence), `apply()` (fills a candidate and **never replaces what a person typed** — `_ROM_KEEP` + `COALESCE`), `game_facts()`, `confidence_words()`, `also_listed_as()`. A catalogue answer is information, never a gate |
| `pi/api/ark_romdb_build.py` + `pi/bin/ark-romdb-build` | the only builder of `/srv/ark/romdb.sqlite`: `--fetch` (needs a connection), `--from-dir` (a carried checkout), `--status`, `--self-test`, `--quiet`, `--out`. `.partial` → rename, and the file is handed to the folder's owner because the pages run as `pi` |
| `pi/api/ark_review.py` | the eight emergency topics and the record of what was **read**: `TOPICS` (key, plain name, the page behind it), `looks_there()` (is that page really on the card?), `record()`/`forget()`/`days_since()`/`summary()`/`status()`. A topic whose page is missing is reported as missing, never as ready |
| `pi/api/ark_household.py` | the nine answers a household writes down: `ENTRIES` (key, question, hint, limit, lines), `clean()` (trim, control characters out, a refusal that names the limit — never a truncation), `load()`/`save()` (settings rows), `filled()`, `missing()`, `progress()`. Read at call time, always escaped when rendered |
| `pi/api/ark_cards.py` | the three wall cards (`cards()`) and the three addresses (`addresses()`) — the only list of names the household is told to try |
| `pi/api/ark_qr.py` | the only QR encoder on the box: byte mode, automatic version, Reed–Solomon, inline SVG. **No dependency**; `qrcode`/OpenCV are a cross-check the tests use when they happen to be installed |
| `pi/api/ark_essentials.py` | what a pack **is**: `collect()` (the vault pages worth carrying — **never** the vault's root `index.html`, which phase 4 generates and the pack replaces with its own front page — plus the quick card and the recovery sheet), `_rewrite()` (a `/vault/…` link stays inside the pack), `build()` (`.partial` → rename, nothing written outside the pack, and a `ValueError` **before** any file exists when two members would share a name), `verify()`, `status()`, `names()`; `ARK_ESSENTIALS_DIR`/`ARK_ROOT` read at call time |
| `pi/bin/ark-essentials` | the only writer of a pack: `--build`, `--status`, `--list`, `--verify`, `--out DIR`; reads `/srv/ark/vault` and never writes it |
| `pi/api/ark_self_heal.py` | **the only thing that decides what a power cut broke**: `fix_clock()` (the newest mtime on the card is the evidence; `CLOCK_SLACK = 120` so a file written a moment ago is never proof the clock is wrong), `heal_json()` (small files: set aside as `…corrupt-<stamp>`, the `.bak` back **only if that reads**), `heal_state()` (**the watchdog's counters, judged by `STATE_KEYS` — `key=value`, never JSON**), `heal_sqlite()` (a read-only `quick_check`; a broken file is replaced only by a copy that passes the same check, and a `-shm` that is simply gone after a cut is healthy), `sweep()` (temp files and `.partial` folders **by pattern and age**, `logs/.*.tmp` included), `heal_library()`, `run()`/`report()`/`main()` (`--dry-run`, `--quiet`, `--json`). Writes `logs/self-heal.json` + `.log`. **Never deletes a database, never restarts a service, takes no copy, touches no unrecognised file.** `healed` = repaired; only `problems` (what is left for a person) exits 1 |
| `pi/api/ark_atomic.py` | the one way a small file is written on the box: `write_json()`/`write_text()` = temp file in the same folder, `fsync`, `os.replace`, and the previous copy kept as `.bak` — so a cut can never leave half a file, and `heal_json()` has something to fall back to |
| `pi/bin/ark-fsck-once` + `pi/systemd/ark-fsck-once.service` | **the one hand that takes the forced card check back off the boot line**: removes `fsck.mode=force` and `fsck.repair=yes` and nothing else, writes **nothing** unless the result is still exactly one line and every other word is unchanged (`root=` may never be lost), and refuses a `cmdline.txt` that already holds more than one line; `--dry-run`, `--quiet`, `--cmdline F`. The unit is `enabled` for ever and idempotent (most boots have nothing to withdraw) and phase `15-resilience` installs it **without starting it** |
| `pi/bin/ark-self-heal` | the command phase `15-resilience` installs to `/usr/local/sbin/ark-self-heal`: `--dry-run`, `--quiet`, `--json`, `--help`; imported by `ark-self-heal.service` (`Before=ark-api.service`) and `ark-self-heal.timer` (04:17 ±20 m, `Persistent=true`) |
| `pi/api/ark_ai.py` | Ask the Ark: the brief built from **what is really here** (counted, never the household's nine answers, the board, the File Box or the accounts), the guide excerpts, the **prompt budget** (`PROMPT_BUDGET`/`BRIEF_BUDGET`/`GUIDE_PAGES`/`GUIDE_EXCERPT`, made of measured speeds — `CHARS_PER_TOKEN`, `READ_TOKENS_PER_SECOND`, `WRITE_TOKENS_PER_SECOND` — so reading does not outrun a person's patience), page retrieval (`MIN_WORD`, `TITLE_WEIGHT`, `TOPIC_HINTS` as a preference and never a filter), `one at a time` as a **lease** — `begin()`/`end()`/`busy_for()`/`cancel()`, never a bare lock (§32) — the stream (and the cancel that stops it when the reader goes), the honest no-brain sentence, the `public_ask` switch, `ask_section()` for `/api/ready` (never blocking). Talks to `127.0.0.1:8089` with `ANSWER_SECONDS`; a box with no model is a supported state |
| `pi/bin/ark-ai-serve` / `ark-ai-model-fetch` / `ark-build-ai-brief` | the server wrapper (waits for a `.gguf`, restarts when a newer one lands, never downloads), the only way a model arrives (`.part` → fingerprint → rename, `--from`/`--url`), and the brief (`--write`/`--dry-run`/`--show`) |
| `pi/api/ark_recover.py` | recovery logic, reached only through the root wrapper |
| `pi/bin/ark_bundle.py` | what a bundle **is**: layout, manifest format 1, path rules (`safe_rel`/`inside`), sha256, `verify_bundle()`, `copy_database()` (SQLite `backup()`, never `cp`); both tools import it so they cannot disagree |
| `pi/bin/ark-backup` | the only writer of a bundle: `--to`, `--keep 2`, `--no-files`, `--dry-run`, `--list`, `--verify`, `--quiet`; `.partial` → rename; never writes to `drop/files`; hands every published bundle (and the `.lock`) to the destination folder's owner, because the pages that report copies run as `pi` and `sudo` would otherwise leave a `root:root` copy they cannot open |
| `pi/bin/ark-restore` | the one deliberate writer of records: proves every byte first, quiesces every writer (`--no-service` for a card in a reader) and restarts what it stopped even on failure, refuses an occupied box without `--force`, never replaces a shared file, keeps the import list off an empty shelf (`.from-backup`), refuses a path that leaves the box |
| `pi/steps/95-backup.sh` | installs both tools + the sheet, creates `/srv/ark/backup` `0700`, proves `--dry-run`, prints the restore command; **no timer, on purpose** |
| `pi/bin/ark-import-books` | the book watcher: scans `drop/files` + `calibre-incoming`, imports once per sha256 into `.imported.json`, moves incoming files to `done/`, copies nothing out of the File Box |
| `pi/systemd/ark-book-import.path` / `.service` | the trigger for the above: `PathChanged` on both folders, `oneshot`, runs as `pi`, 30-minute ceiling |
| `pi/bin/ark-tls-cert` | the box's own certificate: SANs, `--show` fingerprint, renew at 30 days, `--force` |
| `pi/bin/ark-admin-recover` | `0700 root:root`, runs the Python as `pi` (keeps WAL owned by the service user) |
| `pi/nginx/ark-admin.conf` | `:8443` management origin (`/admin` + stylesheet only, unknown Host rejected) and `:8444` loopback-only setup listener; blanks `X-ARK-Setup-Channel` on `:8443` |
| `pi/nginx/ark.conf` | public `:80`; `location ^~ /admin { return 404; }`; and `client_body_temp_path /srv/ark/tmp/nginx 1 2` — a request body parks on the **card**, never in the 256 MB tmpfs `/tmp` became (§28, `docs/ADMIN.md` §13.2). It is set **per `server` block, not once for nginx**: in `ark.conf`'s `:80`, in `ark-admin.conf`'s `:8443` (the sign-in listener that carries the owner's game upload), and in `ark-players.conf`'s `:8445` (a save, and a game added with no sign-in). `:8444` (loopback setup, a password form) and the rejecting default server deliberately have none — a new listener that takes a body must carry the line, or it parks the body in RAM |
| `pi/steps/65-admin.sh` | installs the above and proves each door (404 / 403 / TLS-only, Community and Games behind the sign-in, `:8445` player origin) |
| `pi/steps/90-verify.sh` | read-only health report: every page, every listener, the admin door, the game shelf and player origin, the File Box folders, free space, the network timer + pin + last watchdog decision, the backup tools + newest copy and its age (soft) + the recovery sheet, and the **card check** (`withdrawn` green / `waiting` soft, because a box that has not rebooted since phase 15 is waiting and not broken / `absent` ⇒ run the phase) — `ark_status.json` gains `"card_check"` |
| `tests/` | 523 pytest tests, 2 skipped (the QR cross-checks need `qrcode`/`opencv-python-headless`), throwaway paths and databases, no Pi needed; 24 files |

### 8.2 Ports and who may reach them

| Listener | Serves | Reachable from |
|---|---|---|
| `:80` | the whole public portal | anyone on Wi-Fi or LAN; **404 for `/admin`** |
| `:8443` | `/admin` + `/assets/ark-admin.css`, TLS only | the LAN — but a sign-in is required |
| `:8444` | `/admin` setup only, loopback-bound | only through an SSH tunnel (`ssh -N -L 8444:127.0.0.1:8444 pi@ark.local`) |
| `:8445` | `/arcade/` + `/assets/`, TLS only | the LAN — for player names and saves; `404` for `/admin` |
| `:8081` `:8083` `:8085` `:8089` | kiwix / calibre / API / the helper's engine, proxied by nginx | `127.0.0.1` only — the unit binds loopback, so the proxy is the only way in |

`/household`, `/cards` and `/essentials.zip` are not listeners either — they are pages and one file the app builds, named in `location ~ ^/(search|board|files|household|cards|essentials\.zip)$` on `:80`, so they come off the same door as the front page. Because `location /` ends in `try_files … /index.html`, a **200 proves nothing** for any of them: `60-portal.sh`/`90-verify.sh` prove `/household` and `/cards` by a sentence only that page carries, and `/essentials.zip` by content type (404 is honest, 200 must be `application/zip`, 200 with `text/html` is a failure).

`http://ark.lan/recovery.txt` is not a service: it is `/srv/ark/portal/recovery.txt` served by `location /`, so it comes off `:80` beside the front page — plain text, no sign-in, installed by phase `95-backup`.

### 8.3 State on the Pi

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `/srv/ark/api/data/ark.db` | accounts, sessions, audit rows, notes, files, games/revisions, player names and saves, `settings` (`public_board_post`, `public_uploads`, `arcade_players`, `arcade_auto_install`) |
| `/srv/ark/api/data/ark.installed` | marker **outside** the DB: a wiped DB ⇒ `recovery-required`, never a recreated default |
| `/srv/ark/drop/files/` | published shared files, `0644`, served as `/drop/<stored name>`; the stored name is `<16 hex>-<label>` |
| `/srv/ark/drop/tmp/` | partial uploads (`upload-*.part`), **never served**; swept at startup if older than 6 h |
| `/srv/ark/arcade/games/` | published game files, `0644`, served only through `/arcade/g/<slug>/rom`; written only by `ark_arcade_install.publish_candidate()` |
| `/srv/ark/arcade/saves/<player id>/<game id>/` | a named player's saves, `0700` folders and `0600` files; written only by `player_save_put()`; **never** a static folder. **Named by the two numbers, not by names** (`new_player_save_dir(player_id, game_id)`; proved on the card 26 Oct 2026, log §30 — looking under `saves/<name>/<slug>/` finds nothing). The bytes are in `blobs/<slot-stem>-<sha256>.bin` (one immutable file per content, schema **v6**, §27) and the row names the file; a pre-v6 row (`blob = ''`) still reads from its old slot name. A backup carries the save **and** its blob |
| `/srv/ark/arcade/tmp/` | a download waiting to be proved (`rom-*.part`) **or an owner's upload waiting for confirmation** (`upload-<32hex>.<ext>`, `0600`); swept at startup; never served |
| `/srv/ark/tmp/` + `/srv/ark/tmp/nginx/` | where a half-written **request** waits on the card, because `/tmp` is a 256 MB tmpfs (phase 15) and a 2 GiB File Box upload does not fit in RAM: `client_body_temp_path` in all three listeners parks the body under `nginx/`, and `ark-api.service` carries `Environment=TMPDIR=/srv/ark/tmp` (the first megabyte stays in memory, then the program rolls over to a file on the card). Created by phase `20`, and again by `60`/`65` before `nginx -t`, because `nginx` refuses to start without the parent. **On the card since 26 Oct 2026** (`/srv/ark/tmp` `pi:pi 775`, `/srv/ark/tmp/nginx` `www-data:root 700`), and measured there: a 300 MiB upload left it at 1 MB (nginx streams, `proxy_request_buffering off`) while the app's spooled temp file took the card, not `/tmp` (log §29). **Serves nothing, never swept** — the names belong to two other programs, so a sweep could not tell a request in flight from a leftover |
| `/srv/ark/romdb.sqlite` | the offline game catalogue (`0644`), built by `ark-romdb-build`, **optional**: without it every game is still added, named from its file name. Holds names, makers, years, regions and fingerprints — never a game |
| `/srv/ark/essentials/` | the one pack to carry away (`ark-essentials.zip`), written only by `ark-essentials --build` as `pi`; **16 members** on 26 Oct 2026 (41.1 kB): `MANIFEST.txt`, `RECOVERY-SHEET.txt`, `START-HERE.txt`, `quick-card.html`, `index.html`, `manual.css`, three guides and seven manuals — **not** the three wall cards, which print from `/cards`. A copy that stays on the card is **not** insurance and the household page says so. Nothing serves the folder — `/essentials.zip` reads the one file by name |
| `/srv/ark/ai/` | the helper's engine (`llama-server`, arm64, carried to `/srv/ark/src/ai/`) and `models/*.gguf`, written only by `ark-ai-model-fetch`/`58-ai.sh`; **empty models folder = "not fitted yet"**, a supported state. **On the card since 26 Oct 2026:** `Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf` (940 MB, `1adf0b11…`, the answering one) and `google_gemma-4-E2B-it-Q4_K_M.gguf` (3.3 GB, `923c4c86…`, too slow at 2.3 tok/s — newest wins, so the 1.5B answers). Nothing else on the box reads it except `ark-ai.service` on `127.0.0.1:8089` |
| `/srv/ark/ai/brief.txt` | what the helper is told about this card, built by `ark-build-ai-brief --write` as `pi` — **counted, never remembered**: no household answers, no board, no File Box contents, no accounts |
| `/srv/ark/logs/self-heal.json` + `.log` | what the power-cut pass did: `{status, healed[], problems[]}` for a machine, a line at a time for a person (fsynced, rotated at 256 kB with one previous). An **absent** record means no pass has run yet, never "broken" |
| `/srv/ark/logs/net-watch.state` | **`key=value`, not JSON**: `lost_streak`, `home_streak`, `home_block_until` (+ an `updated=` a person reads). Written only by `ark-net-watch`'s `write_state()` (dot-temp → `mv -f`); judged only by `ark_self_heal.STATE_KEYS` (§26.1) |
| `/srv/ark/calibre-incoming/.imported.json` | import ledger keyed by sha256 — the only reason a re-drop is not a duplicate |
| `/srv/ark/calibre-incoming/done/` | incoming books already in the library; **never rescanned** |
| `/etc/ark/calibre-admin` | Calibre-Web credentials the importer uses over loopback (**not** in git) |
| `/etc/ssl/ark/ark-admin.crt` / `.key` | certificate (0644) and key (**0600 root:root**) |
| `/usr/local/sbin/ark-admin-recover` | the only way back in without a password; console only |
| `/etc/ark/mode.pinned` | the owner's network answer (`home` / `hotspot`); **absent = auto**. Written only by `ark-mode` |
| `/srv/ark/logs/net-watch.log` | one line per decision that acted; a quiet check writes nothing, and **the file does not exist until something is actually decided** — an absent log is not a broken timer (§22.1) |
| `/srv/ark/backup/` | the copies: `ark-backup-<date>-<time>/`, `0700 pi:pi`. A bundle holds the accounts table, so the folder is closed — and a copy that never leaves the card is not insurance |
| `/srv/ark/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt` | the printed page for a card that does not boot; the same file as `/srv/ark/portal/recovery.txt` → `http://ark.lan/recovery.txt` |

### 8.4 Commands that matter

```bash
# tests (Mac, throwaway DBs, never the live library)
python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -r tests/requirements.txt
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -o addopts='--strict-markers' -q    # prints "523 passed, 2 skipped"
# `pytest.ini` already passes -q, so a second -q hides the summary line

# the honest answer, on the box or from the Mac
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://ark.lan/api/ready   # 200 ok, 503 = look
curl -s http://ark.lan/api/ready | python3 -m json.tool             # every part + reason
# /api/health is liveness only and says so; never quote it as a verdict

# the network, without touching the radio
sudo ark-mode status                         # mode + address + who chose it
sudo ark-net-watch --decide auto 0 0 1 0 0 2 0 0    # pure table: no link → to-hotspot
sudo ark-net-watch --dry-run                 # what it sees, what it would do
tail -n 20 /srv/ark/logs/net-watch.log       # what it actually did

# the game shelf (console only; run as pi, never sudo — /usr/local/sbin is not on pi's PATH)
/usr/local/sbin/ark-arcade --status          # games, player names, last check
/usr/local/sbin/ark-arcade --seed            # re-check the pinned first shelf
/usr/local/sbin/ark-arcade --import F --slug S --title T --system gb   # a game you supply
/usr/local/sbin/ark-arcade --discover --auto # what the weekly timer runs
/usr/local/sbin/ark-arcade --list            # suggestions and why each was refused
/usr/local/sbin/ark-arcade --match FILE      # what the box makes of a game file (writes nothing)

# the door anybody on the Wi-Fi can use — no sign-in, add but never replace
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://ark.lan/arcade/add   # 200, or "switched off"
curl -s http://ark.lan/arcade/add | grep -c 'Add a game you already have'

# the offline game catalogue (optional; without it every game is named from its file name)
/usr/local/sbin/ark-romdb-build --status        # present? how many names? how old? (reads, as pi)
sudo /usr/local/sbin/ark-romdb-build --fetch    # a first build: /srv/ark is not pi's to write
/usr/local/sbin/ark-romdb-build --self-test     # a synthetic cartridge: no network, no shelf
/usr/local/sbin/ark-romdb-build --from-dir DIR --out /tmp/romdb.sqlite   # a carried checkout

# the household layer (stage 7 — deployed 26 Oct 2026; the nine answers are still nobody's)
/usr/local/sbin/ark-essentials --status     # is there a pack, how big, when, what is missing (run as pi)
/usr/local/sbin/ark-essentials --list       # what is inside it
/usr/local/sbin/ark-essentials --verify     # recompute every fingerprint in the pack
sudo -u pi /usr/local/sbin/ark-essentials --build   # write it — the only writer
curl -sI http://ark.local/essentials.zip | head -n 3  # 404 = no pack yet; 200 must be application/zip
curl -s http://ark.local/household | grep -c 'The page for the day something goes wrong'

# a power cut (phase 15-resilience — code + test only, NEVER RUN ON THE BOX)
sudo /usr/local/sbin/ark-self-heal --dry-run    # what it would repair; changes nothing
sudo /usr/local/sbin/ark-self-heal --json       # the whole report, one JSON object
tail -n 40 /srv/ark/logs/self-heal.log          # what the last pass actually did
cat /srv/ark/logs/self-heal.json                # {"status": …, "healed": [...], "problems": [...]}
systemctl status ark-self-heal.service ark-self-heal.timer --no-pager
systemctl show -p RuntimeWatchdogUSec            # the hardware watchdog, 15 s
# the forced card check belongs to one boot (§28) — every boot after it must be fast
grep -o 'fsck[a-z.=a-z]*' /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt || echo 'gone — this boot used it'
systemctl is-enabled ark-fsck-once.service       # enabled for ever (a one-shot would lose the next check)
journalctl -b -u ark-fsck-once --no-pager | tail -5   # "Withdrew …" or "nothing to withdraw."
# where a 2 GiB upload waits on its way in (never served, never swept — §13.2 of docs/ADMIN.md)
du -sh /srv/ark/tmp /srv/ark/tmp/nginx 2>/dev/null
systemctl show -p After ark-api.service          # local-fs.target — the portal never waits for the internet
systemctl show -p Environment ark-api.service    # TMPDIR=/srv/ark/tmp (its /tmp is a 256 MB tmpfs)

# the helper (phase 58-ai — fitted on the card since 26 Oct 2026, log §31)
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://ark.local/ask   # 200 even with no model
curl -s 'http://ark.local/ask?q=how+do+I+make+water+safe'        # ~100 s: an answer, not a 504
curl -sN 'http://ark.local/api/ask/stream?q=how+do+I+store+water' # chunked, words as they are written
sudo ark-ai-model-fetch --status                 # which model answers (newest *.gguf wins)
/usr/local/sbin/ark-build-ai-brief --dry-run     # what the helper would be told; writes nothing
ls -l /srv/ark/ai/models/                        # empty is normal — "not fitted yet"
systemctl status ark-ai.service --no-pager       # active here; enabled+inactive with no model
journalctl -u ark-ai -n 40 --no-pager | grep 'tokens per second'   # the box's real speed

# push (never force, never move a tag)
GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=20' \
  git push origin feature/admin-hardening

# deploy / verify on the Pi (only with the owner's explicit go-ahead)
# 30-calibre ships the new importer binary and enables ark-book-import.path
# 70-network installs/enables ark-net-watch.service + .timer
sudo bash /srv/ark/src/install-ark.sh --only 20-storage,30-calibre,60-portal,65-admin,70-network,95-backup
sudo bash /srv/ark/src/install-ark.sh --only 90-verify
cat /srv/ark/ark_status.json

# copies of the records (a copy on the same card is not insurance)
sudo ark-backup --to /srv/ark/backup && sudo ark-backup --list
sudo ark-backup --verify /srv/ark/backup/ark-backup-<date>-<time>
sudo ark-restore --list                      # what could be put back

# the owner's door
ssh -N -L 8444:127.0.0.1:8444 pi@ark.local    # first claim only
sudo ark-tls-cert --show                      # compare with the browser
sudo ark-admin-recover --list                 # locked out (console only)
```

Bootstrap account: `admin`, `must_change=1`, claimable **only** through the loopback listener while unclaimed. Its one-time password is generated at first start into `/srv/ark/api/data/bootstrap-password` (`0600`) and is **not** in git; `ARK_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` is only the test/automation hook.

### 8.5 Defects: what stages 3–6 and ARK Arcade fixed, and what is still open

Fixed on `feature/admin-hardening` (each one now has a test that fails without the fix):

* Upload names collide — replaced by one write path in `pi/api/ark_storage.py`: a 128-bit random
  stored name, `os.link` so a collision is impossible rather than unlikely, spool in
  `/srv/ark/drop/tmp` (never served), cap enforced while streaming, 512 MiB reserve, empty-file
  refusal, orphan-temp reconcile at start. The display name is only a label.
* Search "12 s" deadline drained by `shutdown()` joining every worker — one process-wide pool
  (`SEARCH_WORKERS = 6`) plus `future.result(timeout=…)` over the unfinished set only.
* nginx `4g` vs API 2 GiB — `client_max_body_size 2064m` (2 GiB + 16 MiB framing), and
  `tests/test_config.py` keeps the two numbers tied to `ark_storage.MAX_FILE`.
* `kiwix-serve` on `*:8081` — the unit now binds `127.0.0.1:8081`.
* Imports duplicated on every rescan — ledger keyed by sha256 in
  `/srv/ark/calibre-incoming/.imported.json`, `done/` never rescanned, File Box copies never moved
  (the download link survives).
* No moderation, no switch — `/admin/community`: two switches, pin/sorted-out/hide for notes,
  hide for files, delete behind a password re-auth; every action audited, hidden items stay in the
  database, reversible except delete.
* A green dot that could not be wrong, and `/api/health` answering `{"ok": true}` whatever the
  box was doing — readiness now lives in `pi/api/ark_health.py`: **ready = content present *and*
  the loopback service answered**, with a `reason` and an `action`; `/api/ready` answers 200/503;
  `/api/health` is liveness only and says so in its own body; `/admin/health` is the owner's page.
  `content` (anything on the card yet?) is what tells "still filling up" from "broken".
* Progress that was one percentage and nothing else — `ark-zim-download` now measures its own
  rate (byte delta over elapsed time) and `ark_health.progress()` reports `overall_percent`,
  `remaining_bytes`, `rate_bps`, `eta_seconds` and `stalled`. Unmeasured ⇒ `None`, never 0.
* The network was decided once, at boot — a home Wi-Fi that disappeared from under a running box
  left it unreachable until a human rebooted it. `pi/bin/ark-net-watch` now asks the same question
  every minute under `ark-net-watch.timer`: two lost checks ⇒ `ark-mode hotspot --auto`; home back
  in range, three quiet checks and **nobody on the hotspot** ⇒ home again; 30-minute back-off after
  a return that failed. It never overrules a pin (`/etc/ark/mode.pinned`) and never drops a
  connected reader — an uncountable station list (`-1`) counts as "somebody may be there".
* Free space was one number with no idea where it went — `ark_health.storage_breakdown()` measures
  five folders (`Offline Library`, `Books`, `Manuals & Guides`, `File Box`, `Settings and records`)
  plus `card_facts()` for the card total and used; **What is working** renders it with a
  plain-English summary and lists only non-empty folders. Unmeasurable ⇒ `None`, never 0.
* A backup nobody had read back — one dated bundle now has a sha256 manifest, is built as
  `.partial` and published by rename, is proved by `--verify`, and is put back only by
  `ark-restore` after it proves every byte and refuses an occupied box. The printed recovery
  sheet is the page that still works when the box does not.
* A game shelf that could trust a browser or a stored path — the player is vendored (no external
  asset), player pages live on the separate `:8445` origin, the public `:80` location blanks
  `X-ARK-Player-Origin`, player tables/cookie cannot reach `/admin`, and a stored game filename
  that is not a bare name is refused by `rom_path`, `_check_game` and the ROM route.
* **The book shelf was a management console** (R3, `docs/REQUIREMENTS-MATRIX.md`) — `/books` was
  proxied wholesale, so Calibre-Web's admin, account, upload and delete routes were reachable from
  the portal. `pi/nginx/ark.conf` now has two doors on `/books`: a 404 for every management and
  account route (named from the installed 0.6.27 `url_map`, 231 rules) and `limit_except GET`
  for the rest. Reading, search, OPDS, covers and downloads are untouched; the importer is
  unaffected because it talks to `127.0.0.1:8083` directly. `tests/test_config.py` compiles the
  shipped regex and runs it against the transcribed inventory in both directions. **Proved on
  the box** on a throwaway `:18080` listener (log §14), and the same answers were proved **on
  the live listener** by `60-portal`'s `must_be` proofs during the deploy (log §15).

**Still open, and they are deliberate limits, not oversights:**

* Public board posting and File Box uploads are **still anonymous** — the switch turns writing
  off, it never identifies who wrote. There are no per-person quotas, no quarantine and no trash.
* nginx now agrees with the app on 2 GiB, but nothing tests a real 2 GiB upload over the wire. A
  **64 MiB** file has crossed the public games door and been thrown away (§22.2); the File Box's
  much larger door is still unproved.
* Readiness looks; it cannot change. There are no pause/resume *buttons* (the `ark-zim-pause`
  file still pauses the queue) and no reason recorded for the last import. The storage breakdown
  measures the folders it knows about; it cannot change them either.
* The watchdog acts on what NetworkManager and `iw` report. If `iw` is missing or the radio is
  busy broadcasting, `ap_clients()` answers `-1` and the box deliberately stays on the hotspot
  rather than risk cutting off a reader.

Which test proves which fix (useful when a future change breaks one):

| Behaviour | Test file |
|---|---|
| unique stored names, no overwrite, empty refusal, cap, reserve, temp sweep, a row whose file has gone | `tests/test_storage.py` (16) |
| pin / sorted out / hide / restore, both switches, re-auth delete, CSRF, allowlist | `tests/test_board.py` (16) |
| one import per sha256, renamed re-drop, `done/` skipped, File Box link kept | `tests/test_import.py` (7) |
| the deadline holds when one collection is slow, and the pool survives a repeat | `tests/test_search.py` (8) |
| nginx `2064m` still equals the app's 2 GiB, the book door's route list in both directions, the player's box and the catalogue built beside the bundles, **no serving unit waits for the internet**, a buffered body names the card in every listener that takes one | `tests/test_config.py` (30) |
| schema version, migration/upgrade path, the Pi password vector | `tests/test_db.py` (7) |
| empty vs broken vs ready per section, a full card, measured progress, `/api/ready` 503, liveness-only `/api/health`, the owner page, the storage breakdown, a missing or stale copy, a copy the pages cannot open, a File Box row whose file has gone, an unreadable folder | `tests/test_health.py` (26) |
| the 14-case decision table, a fake `nmcli`/`iw` observer, pins, back-off, the timer/unit files, wiring to `ark-mode`, **the counters written temp-then-`mv` and never `>"$STATE"`** | `tests/test_network.py` (30) |
| a bundle round trip, a damaged copy refused, `.partial` swept, rotation, `--force` on an occupied box, a shared file never replaced, a path escape refused, a named player's save carried, the sheet's plain words, a root-made bundle handed to the box (and not `chown`ed when run as `pi`) | `tests/test_backup.py` (22) |
| guest vs named saves, origin header, hidden game, export/import, owner Games page, hide/show, suggestions, the owner's upload door | `tests/test_arcade.py` (34) |
| licence recorded not refused, language table (English / wordless / unrecorded), a GBA entry fetched from its own repository, per-console allowlist, redirect, archive, file shape, seed, a carried shelf installed from a folder, discovery, auto-install switch | `tests/test_arcade_install.py` (36) |
| a visitor adds but never replaces, the same bytes twice, a file the box cannot play, an empty file, a token that is not ours, changing your mind, the door closed, the owner's switch, the typed name decides | `tests/test_arcade_add.py` (10) |
| a cartridge recognised from its own bytes, header wrappers stripped, no catalogue, a title from the file name, a question about a named file answered about that file | `tests/test_romdb.py` (15) |
| a clock put forward from the newest file, **a file from the far future not trusted with the clock**, a broken small file set aside with its `.bak` back only if it reads, **the watchdog's counters judged as `key=value` and never filed back as JSON**, a database replaced only by a copy that passes `quick_check`, **a database a live writer holds left exactly where it is**, temp files and `.partial` folders swept by age, **a dry run that writes nothing at all — not even a copy on a healthy database**, `healed` vs `problems`, the boot unit ordered before `ark-api`, the phase never leaving `cmdline.txt` at two lines, the forced check withdrawn once and nothing on a second boot, the verifier keeping the old receipt, **the memory folders judged by `/etc/fstab` and not by what happens to be mounted**, **the boot line built apart and proved before the card is touched**, **the clock units the package really ships** | `tests/test_self_heal.py` (69) |
| a save written so a cut cannot leave half of it, the same bytes stored once under `blobs/<slot>-<sha256>.bin` with two revisions, an old `blob = ''` row still read from its slot file, a save that could not be recorded leaving the last one readable, the replaced save forgotten only after the record moved, a blob no row names cleared once it is old, a record naming a file outside its folder refused, a listing that hands out no name on the card, the export zip holding the bytes the records name | `tests/test_durability.py` (15) |
| one answer at a time, no model ⇒ one honest sentence, the brief counted from what is really here (**never** the household's nine answers), both doors closed by `public_ask`, the stream never buffered, a carried model verified by fingerprint, `ask_section()` never blocking readiness, **an unfitted helper reporting `content: False`** (not "content that cannot be served"), **a prompt that fits the box's measured reading speed**, **the worst case (full prompt + full answer) inside `ANSWER_SECONDS`**, **a plain word like "cut" reaching the first-aid page**, **a title match worth more than a body match**, **a hint that prefers a page and never hides one**, and **a run of numbered steps broken into one line each**, **a lease that names its holder, is taken back when it is left behind, and cannot be freed by a late answer**, **a reader who goes away freeing the slot the moment they go** (the route driven the way Starlette drives it: one piece, then a close), **the engine unhooked when the reader goes**, and **a page that never asks twice behind its own question** | `tests/test_ai.py` (44) |
| the eight topics each naming the page behind it, a topic whose page is missing reported as missing, the nine answers trimmed/refused with the limit named, three SVG cards (and an optional real detector), a pack that refuses two files of one name, `/household` and `/cards` proved by a sentence | `tests/test_household.py` (13), `tests/test_household_page.py` (34), `tests/test_qr.py` (11), `tests/test_essentials.py` (24) |
| the claim door, forced password change and one-time passwords, throttling per account and per address, session expiry and revocation, forged forwarding headers, the last-admin rule, the TLS probe helper and the doubled-quote check | `tests/test_auth.py` (19), `tests/test_accounts.py` (14), `tests/test_tls_probe.py` (16) |

### 8.6 Delivered and not built yet

Stage 3 delivered: upload safety (unique stored names, one write path, cap + reserve, empty-file
refusal, temp reconcile), import idempotency (sha256 ledger, `done/` exclusion, preserved File Box
links), board moderation + the two Community switches, and bounded search.

Stage 4 delivered: one readiness module (`pi/api/ark_health.py`) that names the reason, `/api/ready`,
`/admin/health`, `/api/health` reduced to liveness, and measured download progress (rate, bytes left,
ETA, stalled).

Stage 5 delivered: `pi/bin/ark-net-watch` + `ark-net-watch.service`/`.timer` (the network is asked
about every minute after boot, not only at boot), `ark-mode` pins (`home`/`hotspot` pin, `auto`
unpins, `hotspot --auto` leaves none), phase `70-network` installing and proving all of it,
`90-verify` reporting the timer, the pin and the last decision, and a measured storage breakdown
with card facts on **What is working** and in `/api/status`.

Stage 6 delivered: `pi/bin/ark-backup` / `pi/bin/ark-restore` on one shared definition
(`pi/bin/ark_bundle.py`), `pi/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt` served as `http://ark.lan/recovery.txt`, phase
`95-backup`, a **Latest copy** readiness row (`ark_health.backup_section()`, never blocking), the
verifier reporting copies and their age, and `tests/test_backup.py` (13 at that checkpoint; 14 at
the tip). **Deployed 25 Oct 2026 (§8.12a)**; one bundle written on the box and verified whole, no
restore that replaces records yet: §8.10.

ARK Arcade delivered after stage 6: the public shelf `http://ark.lan/arcade/`, a vendored
EmulatorJS player, optional named-player saves on the separate `:8445` TLS origin, the owner
**Games** page, schema v3 tables, `ark-arcade-discover.timer`, and named players' saves carried by
`ark-backup`/`ark-restore`. Tests: `tests/test_arcade.py` (26), `tests/test_arcade_install.py`
(35), and one backup save case. **Deployed 25 Oct 2026 (§8.12a)**; since 25 Oct the shelf
holds **23 curated games** (10 GB, 6 GBC, 6 GBA, 1 NES), fetched from the per-console
catalogues and served — but **unplayed on the box**: no game has been played there yet (§8.11).

Stage 7 delivered and **deployed 26 Oct 2026** (`docs/IMPLEMENTATION-LOG.md` §23 for the code,
§24 for the deploy): the household page, the eight emergency topics with a record of what was
read, the three wall cards, the essentials pack, the owner's **This household** page, and the
accessibility pass (skip link first in the tab order, one `<main>` per page, visible focus,
`aria-current` in the nav). On the box `/household` and `/cards` answer 200 from nginx, the owner's
page answers 303 behind the sign-in, `/essentials.zip` is 404 until a pack is made, and one pack
has been built by `ark-essentials --build` as `pi` — **41.1 kB, 16 files**, one `index.html`,
`--verify` whole, served as `application/zip` (42,038 B) — which is also how the
duplicate-`index.html` defect was found (§8.7). The pack holds the manuals and guides, the quick
card, the recovery sheet, `START-HERE`, its own `index.html` and `MANIFEST.txt`; it does **not**
hold the three wall cards — those are printed from `/cards`, and three docs that said otherwise
were corrected (§24.4). **Unproven: no phone has scanned a printed card, nothing has been
printed, the pack has not been opened with the box switched off, and no person has filled in the
nine answers** — every sentence on `/household` is still the page's own admission.

Still absent, **do not describe any of it as done**: per-person quotas; quarantine/approval
queue; trash; pause/resume buttons on the owner page; a backup timer, or any backup button on the
owner page; player password recovery, per-player save quota, save trash, or an owner reset/delete
for a player name; a **Check now** button for games (the weekly timer is the only check).
**Never invent a control that does not exist.**

Two page-size facts, so nobody documents them wrongly again:

* the public board **does** paginate — `BOARD_PAGE = 20`, with Older/Newer links and a total;
* the owner's Community page **does not** — it shows the newest 50 notes and the newest 50 files
  (`LIMIT 50`) and says so in its own headings. That is the limit to state, not "the last fifty
  items of the board".

**Never quote `/api/health` as evidence the box is well.** It answers `{"ok": true}` whenever the web process is up, and its own body now says `scope: "backend-process"` and points at `/api/ready` — quoting it as a verdict is the mistake that made stage 4 necessary. `/api/ready` is the honest one (200 = every part that must work does, 503 = look at `blocking`), and `/api/status` carries the same per-section `ready`/`reason` for the old page.

### 8.7 Gotchas that have already cost time

* **`run_commands` batches run in parallel.** A `pkill` or restart races the probe meant to observe it; two servers binding one port in the same batch fail in a way that looks like a code bug. Sequence state-changers first, observers second. **It bit a commit too (25 Oct 2026):** `git commit --amend --no-edit` and `git push` in one batch pushed the **pre-amend** commit, so `origin` held one hash and the working branch another — a force-push would be needed to reconcile, and the rules forbid it. The way out was `git reset --soft <the commit origin has>` (which keeps the amended change staged) and one fresh commit on top; both then pushed fast-forward. Never batch a history rewrite with the push that publishes it.
* **Apostrophes in commit subjects break zsh quoting** — git then reads the message as pathspecs. Write the message to a file and use `git commit -F /tmp/msg.txt`.
* **Tests need peer trust:** a hand-rolled `TestClient` sees peer `testclient`; set `ARK_TRUSTED_PROXIES=testclient,127.0.0.1,::1` (the fixtures already do) or every admin request is a correct 403.
* **`ark_admin` needs `ensure_initialised()`** (the real app does it at import); a bare router mount on an empty directory raises `no such table: admins`.
* **macOS Python 3.9 links LibreSSL**, so stdlib `hashlib.scrypt` is absent — `cryptography` is the fallback. The Pi vector pinned in `tests/test_db.py` proves the two agree.
* Reading must survive a broken admin store: the `CRYPTO_ERROR` path keeps the public portal up.
* **`pytest -q` twice prints no summary.** `pytest.ini` already passes `-q`, so `-q -q` shows dots and no "523 passed" line; use `-o addopts='--strict-markers' -q` when the count is the evidence.
* **macOS has no `timeout` command** (`zsh: command not found: timeout`, coreutils not installed). Bound a command with `gtimeout` if present, or start it in the background with a log file and poll — do not build a chain that depends on `timeout`.
* **`git push` from a non-interactive session can hang on an SSH prompt.** Use `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=20'`; a failed push then reports instead of waiting for a passphrase.
* **SQLite `ADD COLUMN` cannot add `NOT NULL` without a `DEFAULT`.** Schema v2 relies on `DEFAULT ''` / `DEFAULT 0`; that is why an upgraded box has `files.stored = ''` for old rows and every read goes through `_stored_name()`. A new query that reads `stored` directly will produce empty download links on an upgraded card.
* **The upload guard is one function, not a convention.** The cap, the 512 MiB reserve and the empty-file refusal all live inside `ark_storage.spool()`. Replacing `file.file.read` with `shutil.copyfileobj` anywhere would silently drop all three — `tests/test_storage.py` covers `spool()`, not the route, so the route must keep calling it.
* **A timed-out search worker is not stopped.** `_kiwix_one` waits 9 s while `SEARCH_DEADLINE` is 6 s, on purpose; the page returns at 6 s and the late answer is discarded, but that thread still occupies one of the 6 pool slots. Do not shrink `SEARCH_WORKERS` without measuring repeated searches.
* **Readiness has exactly one author.** `pi/api/ark_health.py` decides; `main.py` and `ark_admin.py` only render it. A new "is it ready?" test written anywhere else will disagree with the owner page within a week.
* **`ready` and `content` are different questions.** `content` = is anything on the card yet (`no` means "still filling up", amber); `ready` = content exists **and** the loopback service answered. Collapsing them makes an empty box look broken or a broken box look fine — the exact defect stage 4 removed.
* **Any HTTP status counts as "answering".** A reader that returns 500 is up; a reader whose port refuses is down. `answers()` treats a response as life on purpose, so do not "improve" it into a 200-only check.
* **Probes are cached for 10 s** (`forget_probes()` clears the cache; the owner page calls it). A test that flips a stub server on or off must clear the cache, or it asserts against the previous answer.
* **`ark_health` paths are read at call time** from `ARK_ROOT` / `ZIM_DIR`. Binding a path to a module constant at import time is how `main.py` used to freeze a directory before the tests could point it somewhere else.
* **Downloads never block readiness.** `BLOCKING_SECTIONS` excludes `downloads`; a stalled queue is reported (`stalled: true`) and still leaves the box usable. Do not add it to the blocking set.
* **`ark-zim-download` is the only authority on rate.** It writes the measured byte delta; `progress()` reads it and returns `None` when it was not measured. Never compute an ETA from an assumed speed.
* **A readiness test needs a real listener.** `tests/test_health.py` starts `http.server` on a loopback port so "answering" and "refused" are two states; mocking the socket layer instead would test the mock.
* **`KIWIX_INTERNAL` is a unit setting; `CALIBRE_INTERNAL` is only a code default.** `pi/systemd/ark-api.service` sets the library address explicitly but not the shelf one, so the bookshelf probe reads the built-in `127.0.0.1:8083`. If calibre ever moves port, `ark_health` keeps probing the old one and the owner page says "not answering" while the shelf is fine. Set both in the unit before changing a port; the probe address should never be an implicit default.
* **A number that may be unknown must render as "not measured yet", never as `0`.** `progress()` returns `None` for rate, ETA and bytes-left when nobody measured them, and the front-page strip therefore omits those figures rather than printing zero (`index.html`: `if (dl.eta_seconds)`). `0 %` and `0 B/s` are claims — write them only when they are true. The owner's *What is working* page goes further and prints only the reason, no percentages at all; do not add a figure to it that the box cannot measure.
* **The network decision is a pure shell function on purpose.** `decide()` in `pi/bin/ark-net-watch` takes nine numbers and prints `stay`/`to-hotspot`/`to-home`; `--decide` exposes it with no radio, no root and no NetworkManager. `tests/test_network.py` drives that table directly (28 collected tests, the 14-case table is parametrized) and separately stubs `nmcli`/`iw` against the observer. Move the table into a "cleaner" place and the test stops testing the shipped script.
* **A station count it could not read is `-1`, not `0`.** `iw` may be absent or the radio busy broadcasting; `ap_clients()` then answers `-1` and `decide()` treats anything non-zero — including `-1` — as "somebody may be there". A `0` there would drop a reader mid-page. Never "fix" `-1` into a zero.
* **A pin suppresses only the return home, never the rescue.** `ark-mode home` / `ark-mode hotspot` write `/etc/ark/mode.pinned`; `ark-mode auto` removes it; the watchdog's own rescue calls `ark-mode hotspot --auto`, which deliberately leaves none — that is how "the box did this" stays distinguishable from "the owner said so". A pin must never be able to leave the box dark.
* **The watchdog acts only through `ark-mode`.** It reads NetworkManager and `iw` and writes a log line and its state file, nothing else; `ARK_MODE_BIN` is the seam the tests stub. Bringing NetworkManager up directly from the watcher would create a second writer to the network, and the two would disagree within a week.
* **`net-watch.log` is quiet by design.** Only decisions that *did* something are logged; a short log is a healthy day, and "the log is nearly empty" is not evidence the timer is dead — `systemctl status ark-net-watch.timer` and `ark-net-watch --dry-run` are.
* **A row and its file are two different facts.** `files` rows and the folder `drop/files` can disagree, and `ark-restore --no-files` is a *supported* way to create exactly that (records back, File Box empty). The download route gates on the row **and** the file; the listing gates on the file too (`main._present()`, which also corrects the number in the heading); the owner's **Shared files** page keeps showing the row so a gone file can be deleted, and its audit line says *“record removed; the file was already gone”*. Never 'tidy' this by deleting rows or files automatically — that is data loss wearing a maintenance costume.
* **A copy's `counts` come from the database, not from the folder.** `manifest.json` records `counts` read from the **copied database** (`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM notes` / `files` / `admins`), so a file dropped into `drop/files` by hand (no `files` row) is copied but not counted — "0 shared files" beside a bundle that holds one is honest bookkeeping, not a bug. A real upload always has a row: `ark_storage.spool()` writes the file and its row together.
* **`--list` answers for the default folder, not the last `--to`.** It lists `ARK_BACKUP_DIR` (`<ARK_ROOT>/backup` on the box); asking about somewhere else is `--to <that folder> --list`.
* **`ark-romdb-build --status` answers about the file it was asked about.** `--out <file> --status` reads *that* file and prints its path; without `--out` it reads `ARK_ROMDB`, else `<ARK_ROOT>/romdb.sqlite`. It used to ignore `--out` and describe the box's own catalogue, which is the `--list` fault one tool over — silent, plausible and wrong. `tests/test_romdb.py::test_a_question_about_a_file_is_answered_about_that_file` holds it.
* **`ark-backup` writes its report to stdout only.** `say()` is stdout, refusals are `Not done: …` on stderr, so `95-backup.sh`'s `head -n 1` parse of the dry run is safe — keep any new progress noise off stdout.
* **A restore is the one deliberate writer of the records.** It verifies every byte first, refuses an occupied box without `--force`, never replaces a shared file already there, parks the import list as `.imported.json.from-backup` when the shelf is missing, and sweeps `.partial` folders rather than listing them. Never teach anything else to "just copy the newest folder" — §6 still names `ark_storage.spool()` as the only writer of `drop/files`.
* **An age the box cannot read is prose, never `0`.** `_age_hours()` uses the manifest's `created`, falls back to the folder's mtime, then to `None` → "at a time the box could not read". `COPY_STALE_HOURS = 168` only makes the row *not ready*; `backup` is not in `BLOCKING_SECTIONS`, so a missing copy can never make a serving box look broken (§8.8 rule 11).
* **A licence is information, and that is a code fact, not a policy note.** `ark_arcade.license_problem()` returns `""` for every input and `ark_arcade_install.rights_facts()` always answers `(True, "")` — both kept so the pages and the tests share one name. Do not reintroduce a refusal for NC/ND, "freeware", "all rights reserved" or a blank licence, and do not re-add a rights-evidence requirement: the owner decided on 25 Oct 2026 that the shelf is built from what is good to play, and the licence text is shown on the game's card as provenance.
* **Language is the only remaining refusal, and only when the source says so.** `language_facts()` refuses a *recorded* non-English language and a recorded conflict; an unrecorded language is allowed, because most homebrew entries say nothing. A test that insists "unrecorded ⇒ refused" is testing the defect that kept 38 of 40 candidates off the shelf.
* **Each console has its own repository, and the prefix is chosen by the resolved system.** GB/GBC → `gbdev/database`, GBA → `gbadev-org/games`, NES → `nesdev-org/homebrew-db` (`SOURCE_RAW_BY_SYSTEM`). A single prefix 404'd every GBA and NES candidate for a day. SNES/Genesis map to `""` — no repository, so `entry_to_candidate()` returns `None` rather than inventing an address.
* **A search answer may omit `platform`.** `_system()` falls back to the extension when the entry names no console (`_EXT_SYSTEM`; `.bin` is deliberately absent because two consoles share it). Without that fallback a perfectly good `.gb` entry was dropped as "cannot be named".
* **`ark-arcade --seed --from-dir` exists because the box may have no internet.** It publishes the pinned shelf from carried files through the same `publish_candidate(..., source=…)` path, so the recorded SHA-256 and size are still enforced: a different file with the right name is refused, and re-running skips what is already there. Do not teach the installer to `cp` into `games/` — that would bypass every check.
* **`ark.lan` is not a name a Mac can look up.** It resolves only while the box is broadcasting (its own dnsmasq answers every name) or on a network whose DNS knows it; `.local` is the only special-use name mDNS answers. So the owner's door is `https://ark.local:8443/admin` on a home LAN and `https://ark.lan:8443/admin` from a phone on the hotspot. `https://<lan-ip>:8443` sends no SNI, hits the management listener's default server and is refused on purpose — that is not a broken door, and the certificate already covers both names.
* **`boot --dry-run` is how a cold boot is proved without rebooting.** `ark-mode boot --dry-run` prints the decision from `BOOT_MODE` and touches nothing (it does not even need root). `BOOT_MODE=hotspot` is the shipped default and it *pins*, so the watchdog will not walk the box back onto a router nobody asked for; `ark-mode auto` releases it. Never "prove" the boot path by switching the radio under an SSH session.

* **An owner-supplied game carries its own fingerprint now; do not remove it.** `owner_candidate()` records `arc.sha256_file(source)`, which is what lets `publish_candidate()` answer *"already on the shelf, unchanged"* and what lets `ark_arcade.game_by_sha()` refuse the same bytes under a second name. Without it the dedup branch is dead code for owner files — that defect put `stars-r2.gb` on the card for a file that had not changed. `--dry-run` computes it too, so a dry run reads the whole file (≤ 64 MB) and proves the path, the extension, the size and the language — **not** the console header, which is only checked inside `publish_candidate()`.
* **The twin refusal names the *earliest* game with those bytes, not the newcomer.** `game_by_sha()` asks `ORDER BY id LIMIT 1`, so copying a shelf game and importing it under a new slug answers *"already on the shelf as "2048""* — naming the original is the useful answer, and that is what the box said on 25 Oct 2026. And a public page for a slug that is not on the shelf answers **200** with *"That game is not here"* while its ROM route answers 404: the page is deliberately friendly. Never read that 200 as "the game exists", and never "fix" it into a 500.
* **`ark-arcade` is run as `pi`, never with `sudo`.** `/usr/local/sbin` is not on `pi`'s `PATH` (that is why `README.md`, `docs/ADMIN.md`, `docs/USING-THE-ARK.md`, `docs/REBUILD-ME-ARK.txt` and §8.4 here all spell the full path), and it does not need root: `/srv/ark/arcade/games` and `/srv/ark/api/data` are `pi:pi`, and both services that also write them run as `pi`. A root-run import leaves root-owned game files and root-written rows — the same fault as the root-made backup bundle the owner page could not read (`copies_unreadable()`). There is **no drop folder**: the File Box does not feed the shelf, a zip is not unpacked, and nothing watches a game folder. The one in-page way in is **A game you already have** on the owner's Games page, which spools to `arcade/tmp` and then goes through the same `publish_candidate()` gate (the console command `--import` still exists).
* **An owner's words reach the game page, so escape them.** `--description` is the line under the title (it used to be *"Ready to play."* for every hand-added game) and `--notices` is the credits line under the facts — both rendered through `arc.esc()`, because a person typed them. `notices` was stored for a month without ever being shown; if a field like it is added to the shelf, render it or stop offering the flag.
* **`ark_db.tx(conn)` is nested-aware now; do not open a second writer around it.** The shelf publishes a save row and a file together, so `tx()` accepts a connection the caller already holds and turns into a no-op when that connection is in a transaction. Before this, `with ark_db.tx() as conn: ... arcade_save(...)` opened a second `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` and deadlocked with `database is locked`. The `tx(conn)` call shape still works; `tx(immediate=False)` still works.
* **The arcade's app files and the card's data are two different folders.** `src_root()` (`ARK_ARCADE_SRC`, `/srv/ark/src/arcade`) holds templates, CSS, seed list and the vendored player; `data_root()` (`ARK_ARCADE_DATA`, `<ARK_ROOT>/arcade`) holds `games/`, `saves/` and `tmp/`. Binding one to the other puts the player in the writable data folder or the saves in a read-only source tree.
* **`X-ARK-Player-Origin` is a trust boundary, not a hint.** `:8445` sets it to `1`; the public `:80` location sets it to empty, because nginx would otherwise pass a visitor's invented header through. `ark_arcade_web._player_origin()` is the only reader; a new route that returns a save or sets a player cookie must call it, or it will do so through the public portal.
* **Python 3.9 is still the floor for `pi/`.** An f-string cannot contain a backslash in its expression part; `f"{json.dumps(v).replace('</', '<\\/')}"` is a `SyntaxError` on the Mac's Python and on the box. Precompute it (`_js()` in `ark_arcade_web.py`) and test with `python3 -m py_compile`.
* **A player cookie is `Secure`.** It is issued only on `:8445` and deliberately does not travel over plain HTTP; the public shelf therefore shows a signed-in reader as a guest and sends **My saves** to `https://<host>:8445/arcade/player`. A public page must never render the save panel merely because a cookie was present.

* **A row can outlive its file, on purpose — so the pages filter, never the database.** `ark-restore --no-files` puts the records back on a card whose File Box is empty, and a folder tidied by hand leaves rows behind; both are supported, not corrupt. `ark_storage.present_names()` is the one reader of "what is really in the folder", the listing (`main._present()`) and the owner page (`ark_health.files_section()`) both count only what a reader can open, and **neither deletes anything** — deleting a record or a file to tidy a listing is data loss wearing a maintenance costume. The owner's **Shared files** list is where a leftover is seen and removed, with an audit line that already says *"the file was already gone"*.
* **`present_names()` answers `None`, not an empty set, when the folder cannot be listed.** An unreadable folder is not an empty box: the listing shows the rows unfiltered and `files_section()` reports `files_missing: None`. A `0` there tells the owner nothing was ever shared.
* **`sudo ark-backup` writes as root, and the pages that report copies run as `pi`.** A `root:root 0700` bundle inside the `pi:pi 0700` copy folder is unreadable to `ark_health`, which then said "no copy has been made yet" beside a copy that was sitting right there. `ark-backup` now gives each published bundle (and its `.lock`) the **destination folder's owner** (`_give_to_owner()`/`_adopt()`, root only, never fatal), phase `95-backup` adopts bundles that already exist, and `copies_unreadable()` makes a copy nobody can open a sentence of its own — never "no copy" (§8.7's `None` rule, one folder over). `ark-restore` already had the same idea, chowning restored files to the `pi` account unless `--keep-owner`.
* **A TLS probe must ask by NAME.** A request to `https://127.0.0.1:8443/…` carries no SNI, so nginx serves the default server — and the management listener's default server answers `ssl_reject_handshake on`, so the connection dies and curl reports `000` on a healthy door. That failed phase `65-admin`'s own proof on the box on 25 Oct 2026. Use `ark_tls_code <host:port> <path>` from `pi/lib/common.sh` (it adds `-k --resolve "<host>:127.0.0.1"`); never write a TLS probe of your own in a step — the first fix went into `65-admin` only and `90-verify` then failed twenty minutes later with the same `000`. Plain-HTTP probes are the opposite and must keep the address literal: they are asking the port, not the name.
* **`bash -n` cannot see a doubled quote.** `x="$(cmd)""` leaves a string open; if any later line closes it the file stays "balanced", `bash -n` passes, and the assignment swallows whole lines — the script then dies at runtime with something like `adm_code: unbound variable`. That is exactly how a patch of mine broke `90-verify` on 25 Oct 2026. `tests/test_tls_probe.py` now fails if any line of the three shell files ends with `)""`. When Bash "passes" but a variable is unbound, look for a stray quote before looking for a logic bug.
* **The book door is a named route list, and a GET rule cannot replace it.** `limit_except GET` in front of Calibre-Web refuses every POST/PUT/DELETE, but `/admin/*`, `/login`, `/me`, `/generate_auth_token/<id>`, `/shelf/create` and the admin-only `/ajax` endpoints are reachable with GET alone — they are named individually in the 404 door in `pi/nginx/ark.conf`. Do not "simplify" them into `location ^~ /books { return 404; }` or drop one of them: `tests/test_config.py` compiles that exact regex out of the shipped file and runs the transcribed 0.6.27 inventory through it in both directions. `/books/ajax/listbooks` and `/books/ajax/book/<uuid>` are deliberately left open (the table view pages with them) and `/books/advsearch` is the single POST that only reads. When Calibre-Web is upgraded, re-enumerate `calibreweb/cps/*.py` and change the config, the test and `docs/REQUIREMENTS-MATRIX.md` in one commit; when the shelf page starts using a new route, add it to `READING` and to the installer's `must_be` proofs.
* **The player needs a box with a height, and that box must be OURS.** `#arcade-frame` carries the aspect ratio and `#arcade-stage` fills it. EmulatorJS writes `--ejs-primary-color` into **its own element's `style` attribute**, erasing anything inline we put there; and with no height anywhere the canvas is `height:100%` of nothing, collapses, and the game runs perfectly and invisibly — `getState()` still returns 35 KB, no error, no message, no console warning. That is what "the player does not load" was on 25 Oct 2026. Guards: `tests/test_config.py::test_the_player_box_has_a_height_to_fill`, `tests/test_arcade.py::test_the_player_is_sized_on_a_frame_the_emulator_cannot_overwrite`. Do not "simplify" the frame away.
* **A game must never start itself — the page waits for one tap (the *second*, white-screen defect).** `EJS_startOnLoaded: true` starts the core the instant the page loads, with **no gesture** from the person in front of it; the browser then keeps the page silent (the *"AudioContext was not allowed to start"* warning the console had been printing all along), the core's main loop never ticks, and the canvas keeps its initial WebGL clear — **white**. It looked exactly like a broken player: every URL 200, `90-verify` green, and `getState()` answering 35 KB, because a core can hold a state without running a frame. Following the link from the previous page does **not** count as the gesture: activation belongs to the touched document, so a phone always got the white box while a test that clicked first did not. `ark_arcade_web.game_page` therefore sets **no** `startOnLoaded`; EmulatorJS shows its own **START GAME** button and that tap is the gesture. Proved by asking the emulator, not HTTP: two save-state hashes 4 s apart were **identical** with the page untouched and **different** after a tap — then, with the fix, `started: true`, canvas 622×560 and the 2048 title screen read back out of a screenshot. Guards: `tests/test_arcade.py::test_the_game_waits_for_a_real_tap_before_it_starts`. Never re-add `startOnLoaded`, and never "prove" the player with `curl` or with `getState()` alone.
* **A file route must answer `HEAD`.** FastAPI's `@router.get` answers HEAD with **405 `Allow: GET`** — Starlette's automatic HEAD does not apply to `APIRoute`. EmulatorJS asks for a game's size with a HEAD *before* it downloads, so every file-serving route here is `@router.api_route(..., methods=["GET", "HEAD"])`: `/arcade/g/<slug>/rom`, `/arcade/emulator/<path>`, `/arcade/static/<name>`, `/arcade/notices.txt`, `/drop/<name>`. A test proves HEAD is 200 with the right `content-length` and an empty body.
* **The library catalogue must be built beside the ZIMs it describes.** `kiwix-manage` records each path relative to the folder holding the library file: built in `/tmp` and moved to `/srv/ark/kiwix`, every path read `/srv/ark/srv/ark/kiwix/…`, so the viewer answered 200, OPDS listed every book, and search answered `<error>Content not found</error>` for every handle — for a day, with nothing logged. `pi/bin/ark-kiwix-library` now builds `mktemp "$ZIM_DIR/.library-XXXXXX"` and publishes by rename. It runs as **root** (downloader) and as **pi** (service), so it hands the file to the folder's owner; a `flock` file in that folder took the whole library down with `library.xml.lock: Permission denied` on the first attempt. Guard: `tests/test_config.py::test_the_library_catalogue_is_built_beside_the_bundles_it_describes`.
* **A collection's search handle is its ZIM's file name without `.zim`.** `…_en_all_2026-10` → 25 items; the `id` (a UUID) → 0; the `name` attribute (`…_en_all`, no date) → 0, all of them silently. `ark_health.library_books()` carries `stem` (from `path`, never from `name`) and `main._handle()` prefers it. And the endpoint is **`/kiwix/search`**, not `/search` — the bare path answers kiwix's own 404 page, whose body says "Content not found" and is indistinguishable from a failed lookup.
* **An HTTP 200 is not evidence that a page works.** Both 25 Oct defects were 200 everywhere: every asset, every route, `90-verify` green, `/api/ready` 200. What found them was a headless browser (`/tmp/pwbrowser` + `playwright`) driving the real page over `http://ark.local` and reading the emulator's own `getState()`/`getSaveFile()` — which showed a running core and a black screen at the same time. When a page "does not work" but the network is clean, ask the page, not `curl`.
* **A probe that follows redirects cannot tell a door from a refusal.** The on-box upload proof (§8.9 item 0a) first reported *"signing in is accepted"* for a sign-in that had **failed**: `urllib.request.urlopen` follows a 303 by default, so the 303 to `/admin/login` came back as a 200 carrying the sign-in page, and every later admin request was a correct 403 with *"Not signed in"*. Any probe of an admin door must ask for the redirect itself (`HTTPRedirectHandler` whose `redirect_request` returns `None`) and assert on the **303 and its `Location`**, which is also how the `?ok=` sentence is read — decoded with `unquote_plus`, because `+` is a space in a query string and `unquote` leaves it alone. Same family as "an HTTP 200 is not evidence"; the failure looks like a broken feature, not a broken probe.
* **Two places must not derive a slug two different ways.** `add_confirm()` checked for a clash with the slug built from the **typed** name while `owner_candidate()` derived its own from the **recognised** one (it prefers `prefill()['title']` when the caller passes no slug) — so noting an upload under a new name answered *“already on the shelf”* about a game the person had never named, and a second homebrew game with the same recognised title could never be added. The confirm step hands the typed slug to the publisher (`facts["slug"] = wanted`) now, and `tests/test_arcade_add.py::test_the_name_the_person_types_is_the_name_on_the_shelf` holds it. When a door pre-checks a name and a library derives one, pass the name through rather than deriving it twice.

* **A temp file's name is part of its contract.** `spool_upload()` was written with `mkstemp`, which produces `tmpab12cd.gb` — a name the startup sweep (which looks for `upload-<hex>.<ext>`), the discard route and the docs all disagreed with, so a waiting upload would have survived the sweep and read as a download. It uses `secrets.token_hex(16)` + `O_EXCL` now: unguessable **and** the one shape everything else expects. If you change a filename pattern, change the sweep, the tests and the words in one commit.
* **A game upload route must not be `async def`.** The first version was `async def` with a blocking file read, which stalls the event loop for as long as a 64 MB file takes; a plain `def` makes Starlette run it in its thread pool. The body is capped by its own `client_max_body_size` in `pi/nginx/ark-admin.conf` so a game never travels through the public `:80` portal.
* **schema v5 is additive and the live database goes aside first.** v5 adds fingerprint and `rom_*` columns with `DEFAULT ''`/`DEFAULT 0`; old rows stay readable and `rom_confidence` defaults to the "unrecorded" value, never to a claim. Any deploy that can run a migration takes `sqlite3 … ".backup '…'"` first (§8.9 item 1) — `60-portal.sh` restarts `ark-api`, which migrates in place and has no downgrade path.
* **The shelf has an `index.html` of its own, and the pack has one too — the Mac's copy of the vault does not.** Phase 4 writes a generated `index.html` at the top of `/srv/ark/vault`; `ark_essentials.build()` writes its own front page under that name. The first pack built on the box (26 Oct 2026) therefore held **two members called `index.html`**: `zipfile` only printed `UserWarning: Duplicate name`, `--list` showed it twice and every unzipper silently kept whichever came last — so the one file a person opens first on a stick could have been the vault's older listing. No test could have caught it, because `pi/vault` in the repo has no `index.html` (it is generated on the card). `collect()` now leaves out the vault's **root** `index.html` on purpose (a page called `index.html` inside a folder is content and still travels) and `build()` raises `ValueError("this pack would hold two files called … — nothing was written")` before the `.partial` exists, which `ark-essentials` prints as *Not done: …*. **Anything that asserts on `pi/vault` alone is asserting on a folder that is not the box's** — the same family as "a pack built on the Mac proves the format, not the door". Guards: `tests/test_essentials.py::test_the_shelfs_own_front_page_does_not_ride_along_beside_the_packs` and `::test_a_pack_that_would_hold_two_files_of_one_name_is_refused`.
* **A pack's contents are a listing, not a memory — read the list against the prose.** On 26 Oct 2026 three docs (`docs/ADMIN.md` twice, `docs/USING-THE-ARK.md`, `docs/REBUILD-ME-ARK.txt`) each claimed the pack carried a `cards/` folder with the three wall cards. It never has: `grep -n cards pi/api/ark_essentials.py` is empty and the design never asked for one. Nothing in `tests/test_essentials.py` can notice a document claiming a member that does not exist, so the check that found it was `ark-essentials --list` read name by name beside the paperwork. What the pack really holds is `MANIFEST.txt`, `RECOVERY-SHEET.txt`, `START-HERE.txt`, `quick-card.html`, `index.html`, `manual.css`, `guides/*` (3) and `manuals/*` (7) — **16 members** on the box on 26 Oct 2026 (41.1 kB). The cards are printed from `/cards` and are deliberately **not** in the pack. When a member is added or removed, change the code, the listing-proving test **and** every doc sentence in one commit; a doc that promises a file the product does not write is the §8.6 fault in another medium.
* **A repair tool is the one program on the box that must never be able to lose anything.** `ark_self_heal` repairs what it recognises, sets aside (never deletes) what it finds broken, and leaves anything it does not recognise **exactly where it is** — a database with no good copy is *left alone*, not replaced and not removed, and a half-written file is `…corrupt-<stamp>`, never `unlink`. It never restarts a service and takes no copy. Every test that looks like a repair test also asserts that the *other* files are byte-identical afterwards. Guard: `tests/test_self_heal.py`.
* **A small file's shape belongs to its writer, not to the folder.** `logs/` holds `ark_status.json` (JSON), `net-watch.state` (**`key=value`**) and `net-watch.log` side by side, and `ark_self_heal` treated all of `logs/*` as JSON — so every boot found the watchdog's counters "broken", moved them to `.corrupt-*` and put a JSON body back, which the watchdog could not parse and silently rewrote. Nothing failed: the box booted, `/api/ready` was 200, and the only symptom was counters that never accumulated (a slower watchdog, never a wrong decision). `STATE_FILE`/`STATE_KEYS`/`state_problem()`/`heal_state()` now judge the counters by **their own** shape, `JSON_TARGETS` names the two files that really are JSON, and **the counters are never filed back under JSON** — a damaged counter file is set aside and the watchdog starts again from zero, which is safe by design (`decide()` treats "no streak" as "stay"). The tests had been using the counters as a JSON fixture, which is why nothing failed: **a fixture that is not the real file's shape tests the fixture.** Guard: `tests/test_self_heal.py::test_healthy_counters_are_left_alone`, `::test_damaged_counters_are_set_aside_and_the_watchdog_starts_again`, `::test_counters_that_are_not_numbers_are_damage`, `::test_missing_counters_are_not_a_fault`.
* **Counters are written in one move.** `write_state()` in `pi/bin/ark-net-watch` writes `net-watch.state.tmp` and `os.replace`s it, so a cut can leave either the old file or the new one and never half of either. The sweep skips `*.state` and only takes dot-temp files older than an hour, and `tests/test_network.py::test_the_watchdog_writes_its_counters_in_one_move` holds both halves.
* **`90-verify` keeps the receipt from before.** It writes `ark_status.json` itself, so it would destroy the only evidence a repair pass had for a file that did not parse — it now copies the previous receipt to `.bak` (atomically, via `ark_atomic`) *before* overwriting, which is exactly what `heal_json()` looks for. Anything else that overwrites a file `ark_self_heal` guards must do the same, or the guard has nothing to restore.
* **`fsck.mode=force` is a one-line edit that can make a box unbootable.** `15-resilience.sh` reads `cmdline.txt`, and refuses to write if the result is not still **exactly one line** — a newline in `cmdline.txt` stops the Pi dead with nothing on the screen, and this repo has no console to notice it. Same rule for `/etc/fstab`: a line is added only if it is not already there, and `nofail` is on it. **And the check belongs to one boot:** left on the line it makes *every* reboot wait for a full check of a card that was already checked. `pi/bin/ark-fsck-once` (run by `ark-fsck-once.service`, enabled for ever, started by phase 15 **never** — starting it during the install would withdraw the check the phase had just arranged) removes exactly `fsck.mode=force` and `fsck.repair=yes`, writes nothing unless the answer is still one line and every other word is unchanged (`root=` may never be lost), and prints `Withdrew fsck.mode=force fsck.repair=yes from cmdline.txt: this boot checked the card, the next one will not check it again.` or, on an ordinary boot, `cmdline.txt carries no forced check — nothing to withdraw.` — so `journalctl -b -u ark-fsck-once` answers the question a month later. Do not "simplify" it into a `sed`: this is the one program whose failure is a box that never boots again.
* **A box with no model is a supported state, not a fault.** `ark-ai.service` is enabled and *inactive* with no `.gguf`; `/ask` answers one honest sentence; `ask_section()` in `/api/ready` is never blocking (and must say `content: False` while nothing is fitted — `90-verify` reads `content` as "there is something here that cannot be served" and **failed a healthy card** on 26 Oct 2026 when this row said `True` about a helper that has never existed here; `content: True` belongs only to words on the card that are not answering); closing `public_ask` closes both the page and `/api/ask/stream`; a missing model must never be blocking; and it must never be *content* either. Do not make a missing model a readiness failure — that would make every box in the field "not ready" on the day it ships.
* **A tmpfs mounted by hand is a state, not a setting.** The live card's `/tmp` was a 3.9 GB tmpfs with **no `/etc/fstab` line** (mounted by hand in an earlier session) and `/var/tmp` was on the card, so `15-resilience`'s old branch — which looked at the *live mount* — said "already live in memory" and skipped its own edit: the next reboot would have put `/tmp` back on the card and nothing would have said so. Decide where the setting **lives** (`grep '^# ARK: throwaway folders' /etc/fstab`), let a live mount be one sentence, and put `nofail` on every line added (log §29).
* **An unanchored `sed` edits every line, and a guard after the write is no guard.** The image ships `cmdline.txt` as 133 bytes ending in a blank line (`…regdom=US\n\n`, `wc -l` = 2), so `sed -i "s/[[:space:]]*$/ $opt/"` appended the option to **both** lines and the file grew a second line of nothing but our options; the old `wc -l > 1` check then fired *after* the write and the phase died with the boot line already damaged. `15-resilience` now installs `ark-fsck-once` **first**, reads line 1, appends there, proves one line + `root=` in a temp file, writes in place, and proves one line again. And `grep -qw 'root='` can never match `root=PARTUUID` (the `=` is followed by a word character) — use a plain `grep -q`.
* **A package's unit is not named after the package.** `systemctl enable fake-hwclock.service` answers `not-found` on every Raspberry Pi OS: Trixie ships `fake-hwclock-load.service`, `fake-hwclock-save.service` and `fake-hwclock-save.timer`. Enable what `systemctl cat` finds and count it, or a healthy box gets a warning for ever.

* **The engine must be at the path the unit names, and *carried*, not built.** `ark-ai.service` has `ConditionPathExists=/usr/local/bin/llama-server`; phase `58-ai` installs that from **`/srv/ark/src/ai/llama-server`**. A perfectly working hand-built binary at `~/llama.cpp-build/build/bin/llama-server` is invisible to the box — that is exactly why `/ask` still said *"The helper is not fitted on this box"* on 26 Oct 2026 with a 3.3 GB model and a compiled engine both on the card (log §31). And because `scripts/03-provision.sh` rsyncs `pi/` with `--delete`, both hops now `--exclude 'ai/llama-server'` — a deploy would otherwise delete the very file the phase tells an operator to carry (`tests/test_config.py::test_the_deploy_does_not_delete_the_engine_it_was_told_to_carry`).
* **A `.part` written by a hand-run `curl` is invisible on purpose.** `ark-ai-model-fetch` writes `…part`, verifies, `chmod 644`s and only then renames; the helper globs `*.gguf`, so a complete 3.3 GB download called `….gguf.part` is not a model. Adopt one through the front door — `sudo ark-ai-model-fetch --from <file>.gguf.part --name <name>.gguf` — which is also what prints the fingerprint worth recording.
* **A proxy that gives up before the page does turns a slow answer into a 504.** `/ask` was served by the block that serves searches (`proxy_read_timeout 120s`) while `ark_ai.ANSWER_SECONDS` was 150, so the first two real questions answered `504 Gateway Time-out` at exactly 2m00s on a box that was working perfectly. `/ask` now has `location = /ask` at **300 s**, and `tests/test_config.py::test_the_helper_gets_longer_than_its_own_ceiling` fails if the proxy becomes the shortest patience of the three.
* **Reading is the expensive half on this box, so the prompt is a budget, not a catalogue.** Measured on the Pi 4 (26 Oct 2026): **6.8 tokens/s** of prompt and **3.2 tokens/s** of answer with a 1.5B model in four bits. Three whole guide pages made the prompt 871 tokens = **128 s before a word was written**. `PROMPT_BUDGET`/`BRIEF_BUDGET`/`GUIDE_PAGES`/`GUIDE_EXCERPT` and `MAX_TOKENS = 160` are derived from those speeds, and `tests/test_ai.py::test_the_worst_case_answer_fits_in_the_time_the_page_waits` does the arithmetic — add context and it fails instead of a person waiting.
* **A model bigger than the box is a five-minute answer, and a thinking model answers with an empty `content`.** Gemma 4 E2B Q4_K_M (3.3 GB) measured **4.4 tokens/s** of prompt and **2.3** of answer, and spent its whole token budget in `reasoning_content` ("Thinking Process: …") with `content` empty — the app's own answer to that is `SORRY_SENTENCE`, correctly. **1–2 GB of four-bit weights is the working range**; `--status` says which model will answer, and newest `*.gguf` wins.
* **A plain word is a route word, and a tie is not decided by the alphabet.** *"What do I do about a deep cut"* routed to `general` (so **no page** was given to the model, and it invented `/firstaid/`), four-letter word matching dropped *cut*/*tap*/*gas*, and a tie between two pages that both mention the word was broken by name — which picked sanitation once and household repairs another time. `ROUTES` now carries the plain words, `MIN_WORD` is 3, `TITLE_WEIGHT` makes a title match worth four body matches, ties sort by name, and `TOPIC_HINTS` lets a plain word **prefer** the page that answers it — a preference only, never a filter, and never for a page that is not on the card.
* **No instruction goes in the brief.** The generated brief ended with *"When a question is about a person's own house, say which page on the box answers it…"* and a small model recited it back as the first sentence of its answer. The rules live in `HOUSE_RULES`; the brief is facts only (counted from what is really here).
* **A slot held by a generator is not a lock, it is a leak.** `ark_ai._SLOT` was a bare `threading.Lock`, acquired by `ask_stream()` and released in its `finally` — but a generator's `finally` runs only when somebody *resumes* it, and Starlette cancelling an abandoned response leaves the worker thread blocked inside the engine's socket. Measured on the card, 26 Oct 2026: a `curl` stopped after 6 s left the API's connection to `:8089` **ESTABLISHED**, and the next question was told *somebody else is asking* — for the whole answer (up to `ANSWER_SECONDS = 200`), and for ever if the answer never ended (log §32). Three things now hold it together: the slot is a **lease** (`begin()`/`end()`/`busy_for()`, `LEASE_SECONDS`, an owner-checked release so a late answer cannot free somebody else's slot, and a takeover of an expired one that prints a journal line); the engine's response is **hooked by token** (`_live`) so `cancel(token)` can `close()` it, which is what interrupts a blocked read *and* tells the engine to stop writing on a box with no spare core; and `/api/ask/stream` polls `request.is_disconnected()`, runs the blocking iterator in a worker thread with `abandon_on_cancel=True` (anyio renamed that flag from `cancellable` — ask the signature, never guess), and calls `cancel(token)` from a `finally`. **A `threading.Lock` must never be the thing that decides whether a person gets an answer.**
* **The page must never ask a second question behind its own first.** The `/ask` script called `form.submit()` when the stream failed, so a browser without `Response.body` (or any hiccup) sent a **second** question while the first was still running — and the second was correctly refused with *somebody else is asking*, which looks exactly like a broken helper. It now checks `'body' in Response.prototype` *before* taking the form over, keeps one request in flight (`asking`), and shows one honest sentence on failure instead. Guard: `tests/test_ai.py::test_the_page_asks_one_question_and_never_a_second_behind_the_first`.


### 8.8 Rules for the next stage

1. Test first, then code, then `pytest` green, then a checkpoint line in `docs/IMPLEMENTATION-LOG.md`.
2. The public reading path must keep working at every commit.
3. No deploy, reboot, `pkill`, or service restart on the live box without explicit permission.
4. Docs change in the same commit as the behaviour — `README.md`, `docs/ADMIN.md`, `docs/USING-THE-ARK.md`, `docs/REBUILD-ME-ARK.txt`, `docs/REBUILD-FROM-BACKUP.txt`, `pi/RECOVERY-SHEET.txt` and `docs/REQUIREMENTS-MATRIX.md` (the per-requirement verdict, whose every row cites the test that proves it) all describe the box and have drifted before.
5. Shared files get written by `ark_storage.spool()` and by nothing else (see §6).
6. New schema steps are a numbered migration in `ark_db.py`, bump `SCHEMA_VERSION`, never rewrite v1, and keep old rows readable (see the `stored = ''` rule in §8.7).
7. The two switches are settings, not code paths: `public_board_post` and `public_uploads`, absent or `1` means **open**. A fresh database must therefore start with the box usable, and a test must prove the closed state still serves everything already written.
8. A probe is cheap, cached and cannot raise. Anything that decides readiness goes in `ark_health` with a timeout, and no reader's page may wait on a service that is down — stage 4 exists because readiness was cheap enough to be wrong, not because it was too slow to check.
9. When a rendered value disappears, say so in the same commit: `/api/health` no longer carries `ok`, so anything outside this repo that polls it must be told to read `/api/ready` (200/503) instead.
10. The network is decided in exactly one place after boot: `pi/bin/ark-net-watch`. It may only act through `ark-mode`, it may not overrule a pin, and it may never take the hotspot away while a device is connected (an uncountable station list counts as connected). Prove any change to it with `--decide`, which needs no radio — never by experiment on the live box.
11. New measurements follow the `None` rule: a folder size, a rate or an ETA the box could not measure renders as "not measured yet", never as `0` (§8.7).
12. A copy only reads. `ark-backup` writes into its own bundle and nowhere else; `ark-restore` is the one deliberate writer of the records and of `drop/files`, and only after proving every byte (§6). No timer takes a copy without an explicit yes, no page pretends to have a backup button, and any wording about copies says out loud that a copy left on the same card does not survive that card dying.
13. The arcade's three write paths are closed sets: `ark_storage.spool()` for `drop/files`, `ark_arcade_install.publish_candidate()` for `games/`, and `player_save_put()` for `saves/`. `:8445` sets `X-ARK-Player-Origin: 1`; the public `:80` location blanks it. A game page must never contain an `http://` or `https://` asset reference, and a stored game filename must be a bare name before it is joined to a path.
14. **A name is a convenience, never a condition.** `ark_romdb`/`ark_rommeta` may suggest a title, a console, a year and a description from a file's own bytes; nothing they produce may refuse a file, and `apply()` may never overwrite a word a person typed (`_ROM_KEEP` + `COALESCE`). A missing catalogue is a supported state — `/srv/ark/romdb.sqlite` is not required for any game to play, and a box with no catalogue must still name every game from its file name. Prove a change with `ark-romdb-build --self-test` (no network, no shelf, no database) and `ark-arcade --match FILE` (writes nothing).

15. **A card is drawn on the page, and the pack opens where it lands.** The wall cards are inline
SVG from `ark_qr.py` — no image file, no font, no CDN — and a card a camera cannot read is
worse than no card, so `tests/test_qr.py` decodes them with a real detector when one is installed
(and skips, never passes, when one is not). `/household` and `/cards` are proved by a **sentence**
and `/essentials.zip` by **content type**: `location /` ends in `try_files … /index.html`, so a
200 on either page can be the front page, and a 200 on the pack can be HTML. A pack is written
only by `ark-essentials --build`, as `pi`, `.partial` → rename, and it never writes the vault.
A household answer is a sentence a person typed: escaped on every page, trimmed, control
characters out, and refused with the limit named rather than silently cut.

16. **A repair may never be able to lose a file that was not already broken.** One tool decides
    what a cut broke (`pi/api/ark_self_heal.py`), it sets aside and never deletes, it replaces a
    database **only** with a copy that passes the same check, it touches nothing it does not
    recognise, it restarts no service and takes no copy, and it is judged by the clock **after**
    the clock is fixed. `healed` (repaired, nothing for anyone to do) and `problems` (what a
    person must look at) are different lists, and only the second one exits non-zero.
17. **A small file is written in one move, by its own writer, in its own shape.**
    `ark_atomic.write_json()`/`write_text()` (temp → `fsync` → `os.replace`, previous copy kept
    as `.bak`) is the one way; the watchdog's counters are `key=value` and are judged by
    `STATE_KEYS`, never as JSON; anything that overwrites a file the repair pass guards leaves a
    `.bak` first. A box with no model is a supported state: enabled, inactive, `/ask` answering
    one honest sentence and `/api/ready` not blocking on it.

18. **The helper answers; it does not know the household.** `pi/api/ark_ai.py` is the only thing
    that talks to the engine, and what it may say to it is a counted brief — **never** the nine
    household answers, the board, the File Box, the accounts or a password. One answer is written
    at a time (`BUSY_SENTENCE` tells the second person so), the engine's words are escaped and
    paragraphised before they reach a page, a question is trimmed and refused with its limit
    named, `public_ask` closes **both** the page and `/api/ask/stream` (which is the one nginx
    route with buffering off), and every failure is one honest sentence rather than a 500. A
    model arrives only through `ark-ai-model-fetch` (`.part` → fingerprint → rename); nothing may
    download one at runtime, and no page may wait on an engine that is not answering.
    **And the one slot is a lease, not a lock** (log §32): it names its holder, it is released by
    its own token only, an expired one is taken back with a line in the journal, and the route
    `cancel()`s the engine's response the moment the reader's connection goes — because a
    `threading.Lock` held by a generator whose reader has walked away is a slot nobody can free, and
    the box then answers every question with *somebody else is asking*.

### 8.9 OUTSTANDING — what is not done yet, in the order it matters

**Blocking a release (nothing is wrong with the code; it is unproven in a human's hands):**

